# The Docker client tars this entire directory and ships it to the daemon
# BEFORE the first instruction in any Dockerfile runs.  Both services use
# `context: .`, so the repo root is that context.
#
# So this file is about what never leaves the host, not only about image size.
# A path excluded here is never read, never transmitted, and never sits in a
# daemon-side build cache -- which is the actual reason .env and reference/ are
# listed, and it holds even though no Dockerfile contains a COPY that would
# have picked them up.  "No COPY reaches it" is not the same guarantee.

# ---- Version control ----------------------------------------------------
.git
.gitignore

# ---- Credentials --------------------------------------------------------
# The `!` re-inclusion is ORDER-DEPENDENT: Docker applies the last matching
# rule, so !.env.example has to come after the .env* rule that would otherwise
# swallow it.  Reversing these two lines silently drops the example file.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ---- Real people's data -------------------------------------------------
# DNC and unsubscribe lists hold live email addresses.  Nothing in an image
# needs them and they must not reach the daemon.
reference/

# ---- Large public downloads ---------------------------------------------
# ~1.5G of TIGER shapefiles and the IEM archive.  The loader reads these at RUN
# time through the ./:/repo bind mount, never from the image, so excluding them
# costs nothing and is the single biggest win in this file.
data/

# ---- Not needed to build or run -----------------------------------------
docs/
planning/
tests/

# ---- Python bytecode and caches -----------------------------------------
# The `**/` prefix is REQUIRED and its absence is silent.  A .dockerignore
# pattern is matched against the whole relative path and `*` does not cross a
# `/`, so a bare `__pycache__/` matches ONLY a directory at the context root --
# scripts/__pycache__/ sails straight through and lands in the image.  Verified:
# without these prefixes, three .pyc files were present in the build context.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/*.egg-info/
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/.venv/
**/venv/
